since his debut in 1962 spider-man has  been one of the most popular superheroes  in the world and he's also one of the  most well known but with countless  spider-man stories built around some  pretty confusing plot points it's easy  to get mixed up let us have the great  responsibility of clearing up a lot of  incorrect things people believe about  spider-man of all the original Marvel  Universe titles the amazing spider-man  is one of the most strongly identified  with Stan the man Lee even though he'd  be a part of all of Marvel's early  string of hits including The Avengers  the x-men the Hulk and more Spidey was  the character he stuck with the longest  scripting the first 100 issues and then  staying on sporadically for another few  years after you know I guess one person  can make a difference  enough said if however you think Stan's  the only one responsible for the thrills  of those early adventures you're  mistaken  most fans are aware that Lee pioneered  the Marvel style of writing in which  he'd collaborate with an artist on a  loose plot then add in the dialogue  after everything else was done what's a  little less well-known however is that  his relationship with artists and  spider-man's co-creator Steve Ditko was  one of the first to deteriorate  according to Marvel Comics the untold  story the latter chunk of Ditko's 38  issue run including the legendary story  that gave the world the iconic image of  spider-man fighting his way out from  under a massive heap of rubble was  produced without Ditko and Lee speaking  to each other at all  [Music]  of course that doesn't mean lien wasn't  a necessary ingredients for spider-man's  early success the fact that he stayed  with the character for almost seven  years after Ditko's departure and kept  on producing some of the best years of  spider-man is a testament to how  important he was to the character and  how well he got along with dick coast  replacement jazzy John Romita the one  thing about spider-man that's always  been true is that underneath that mask  is always Peter Parker just an average  everyday scientific super genius with  problems just like ours you know like  juggling our long-term relationships to  supermodels while making a living off  selfies that's why it's always been  Peter except for all those times it's  someone else while Peter Parker has  certainly been the main man in the mask  at spider-man since Spidey's debut in  1962 he's definitely not the only one  for a while in the 90s spider-man was  instead Ben Riley a clone of Peter made  by a villain called the jackal right  around the time Peter temporarily lost  his powers Reilly was tricked into  thinking he was the genuine article he  dyed his hair blonde got a job at a  coffee shop and took over the role with  a new costume for the entirety of 1996  before eventually dissolving into a pile  of dust  more recently Peter found his own  consciousness overwritten by Doctor  Octopus who took over his life as the  superior spider-man beyond just  variations on Peter Parker's body and  enough alternate universe versions that  they have their own special team there's  an entirely different spider-man in the  Marvel Universe Miles Morales who  stepped into the spotlight in 2018 into  the spider-verse a movie based entirely  on teaching us that anyone could be  spider-man if they never give up  including Gwen Stacy Nic Cage and a  talking pig it can get weird  one of the most intense arguments you  could overhear in a comic book store  back when the first spider-man movie  came out concerned whether spider-man's  web shooters were mechanical or an  organic byproduct of his spider powers  going by the comics though it doesn't  matter what side of the argument you're  on you're wrong either way when Sam  Raimi's film hit theaters one of the few  notable changes that it made to  spider-man's origin story came with the  addition of organic web shooters this  proved to be so controversial that when  the next movies were announced in 2010  the decision to use the comics  mechanical web-shooters literally made  headlines after all he'd never had  organic web shooters in the comics or at  least he didn't have them in the comics  before those movies came out after their  success a 2005 storyline called  the other ended with Peter being eaten  by a giant spider in emerging with  organic webs those didn't last either  they inexplicably disappeared with the  back-to-basics approach of the brand-new  day story two years later but other  Marvel Universe Spider characters  retained them in fact Cyndi moon a  classmate of Peters who was bitten by  the same radioactive spider is so  capable of spinning her own webs that  her super heroic code name became silk  one of the defining tragedies of  spider-man's life was the death of Gwen  Stacy in the 60s she was the love of  Peter Parker's life forming a  complicated love triangle with Mary Jane  Watson and providing the backbone of the  soap opera that underscored his Spidey  super heroics an amazing spider-man  number 121 the Green Goblin kidnapped  her and used her as bait to lure  spider-man into a battle at the top of  the George Washington Bridge and while  the story title wasn't revealed into the  last page the night Quinn Stacy died  lived up to its name the Goblin is  definitely to blame for Gwen's murder  he's the one who took her to the top of  the bridge and the one who threw her off  all in an effort to torture spider-man  if however you really want to get  technical about things he's not the one  responsible for what literally killed  her  that sad honor goes to spider-man  himself it's easy to miss but the real  tragedy can be seen in the issue when  Spiderman tried to save Quinn by  snagging her leg with a web line as she  plunged toward the Hudson River the  sudden stop proved to be the fatal  moment a small snap sound effect next to  Quinn shows it was a broken neck from  Peter's attempt at saving her that  actually ended Quinn's life spider-man  doesn't kill its one of the absolute  rules of the character and unlike other  heroes he's lived up to it too in over  50 years of comics spider-man has never  actually killed anyone himself except  for the one time he did it happened in  the pages of spider-man vs. Wolverine a  1987 one-shot by Christopher priest and  Marc bright that pulled Spidey into a  dark world of international espionage  the conflict between the two title  characters is largely built around a spy  codenamed Charlemagne who is described  as Wolverines best friend from his days  as a government operative in this story  Charlie wants Wolverine to kill her so  she won't be apprehended and spend the  rest of her life being tortured in a  gulag when spider-man stumbles into this  situation all he knows is Wolverine is  trying to kill someone in the fight that  ensues Spidey unleashes his full  strength on the x-man hitting him with  blows that would wreck cars  sadly Charlie takes advantage of the  brutal conflict tricking spider-man into  hitting her instead of Wolverine using  him to commit suicide Peter was  understandably traumatized by the event  and while he and Wolverine have made  peace in the year since it was a source  of bitterness between them for a long  time the iconic image of Peter Parker is  of a young man struggling to make ends  meet by working as a freelance  photographer selling snapshots of his  own super heroic exploits to the Daily  Bugle to satisfy Jay jonah Jameson's  demand for pictures of spider-man if  that's your only concept of what Spidey  does when he's not out fighting crime  however it's time for an update the  truth is while Peter worked for the  bugle for decades in a lot of classic  stories he hasn't actually been a  freelance photographer for most of this  century starting in 2001 Pete returned  to his old stomping grounds of Midtown  high as a science teacher but that  proved to be a pretty short-lived career  choice in the years since he's had a  couple of science related jobs working  for a think tank called horizon laughs  where he reverse engineered his  crime-fighting gadgets into consumer  goods he even founded his own  multinational corporation called Parker  industries although technically it was  Otto Octavius who founded that company  while he was in Peters body and Peter  had to destroy it to keep it from being  taken over by Nazis comics are  complicated  one of the more controversial moments in  spider-man's comics history involved a  deal with the devil like the literal  devil not just this guy  after Peter revealed his identity during  the events of civil war his family was  targeted by bad guys who nearly killed  Aunt May which was a good reminder of  why he had the whole secret identity  thing to begin with unfortunately for  the Parker family that sort of reveal  isn't the kind of thing you can undo  unless you're willing to make an  infernal bargain with Mephisto which is  exactly what happened rather than  selling their souls however Peter and  thin wife Mary Jane Watson sold their  marriage effectively erasing their years  of wedded bliss from Marvel Comics  continuity as though it had never  happened in the spider-man comics Peter  and MJ were both freed up to pursue new  relationships but don't let that fool  you into thinking the marriage is  completely gone in order to go along  with the comic book storyline the  spider-man newspaper strip did away with  the marriage as well  readers reacted so poorly however that  in the strip that rain on May 24 2009  the elimination of the marriage was  revealed to have been a dream ever since  newspaper Spidey has remained happily  married or at least as happy as anyone  can be when they spend most of their  time getting knocked unconscious right  next to Dilbert everyone knows that  Peter Parker was orphaned at a young age  and raised by Aunt May and Uncle Ben  considering that his surrogate parents  were about as wholesome and  salt-of-the-earth as people could be it  stands to reason that Richard and Mary  Parker were cut from the same cloth just  average ordinary folks right wrong in  1968 it was revealed that when the  Parker parents died they were under a  cloud of suspicion as traitors to their  country the truth however was that they  were spies working for shield who had  infiltrated the red skulls criminal  organization before being killed without  ever getting the chance to clear their  names yes in case you missed that  spider-man's parents were spies who were  murdered by Captain America's  arch-nemesis if that's not weird enough  they later turned up alive only to be  revealed even later to be robot  duplicates that's not the end of the  Parker family's secrets though it turns  out that in addition to the parents he  never knew  Spidey also had a long-lost older sister  Teresa who was herself a SHIELD agent  after a team-up in which it was revealed  that her men  of the Parkers were falsely implanted by  Mephisto it turned out that was also a  ruse she was in fact Peters actual  sister after all  and even pitched in to help him fight  bad guys using her spy skills if you've  ever read any spider-man comic or seen  him in a movie or a cartoon or  encountered the character pretty much  anywhere then you've almost certainly  seen the motto that guides his life with  great power comes great responsibility  except Ben Parker never actually said  those words in amazing fantasy number 15  Spidey's often referenced motto is never  spoken out loud by any character not  even Uncle Ben instead it only appears  in a caption at the end of the story as  a moral lesson imparted by well Stan Lee  Spidey himself doesn't even say it until  25 years later in 1987 as time went on  though the phrase became more and more  identified with bhim largely because it  would be really weird for spider-man to  solemnly vow to accept the lesson that  was imparted to him through narration by  the guy who wrote his comics check out  one of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about your  favorite stuff are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Applause]  [Music]
 

